      "id": 1,
      "text": "On February 9, 1790, Stone's growing village was named 'Greensburgh' (later changed to 'Greensboro') in honor of Revolutionary War hero Nathanael Greene, for whom Greene County was also named when it was formed from Washington County in 1796.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greensboro%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Due to its location at the head of the slack waters of the Monongahela, Greensboro's influence as a commercial and manufacturing center expanded far beyond its small boundaries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greensboro%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early in the town's history, pottery became an important part of Greensboro's economic base, with the first operation owned and operated by Alexander Vance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greensboro%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Boughner House, Peters-Graham House, James Parreco House, and James Jones House are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
